PDF (nPDF) effects, Cronin effect and Nuclear absorption. The nPDF effect originates from the modification of the parton distribution functions in the nucleus (nPDFs) relative to the free nucleons. The effect of nPDFs including shadowing, anti-shadowing, EMC and Ferimi motion effects. A ratio of PDFs between nuclear and proton can be used to characterize these effects, which is called the nuclear shadowing functions \(\mathcal{R}_{\rm i}^{\rm A}\) (i denotes parton, \(\rm A\) is the nuclear type), as illustrated in Figure 15. Typically, the shadowing effect corresponds to the small x range (x \(<\) 0.025) with \(\mathcal{R}_{\rm i}^{\rm A}<1\); the anti-shadowing effect corresponds to the intermediate x range (0.025 \(<\) x \(<\) 0.3) with \(\mathcal{R}_{\rm i}^{\rm A}>1\); EMC effect corresponds to the large x range (0.3 \(<\) x \(<\) 0.8) with \(\mathcal{R}_{\rm i}^{\rm A}<1\); and the Fermi motion effect corresponds to the high x range (x \(>\) 0.8) with \(\mathcal{R}_{\rm i}^{\rm A}>1\). The parameterization of nuclear modifications has been performed under the different models: HKN07 [34], EKS98 [35], EPS09 [36]..., an example of nuclear modifications for Lead under EPS09 frame is shown Figure 16.

