At RHIC and LHC energies, the main source for the creation of heavy quark pairs is the gluon fusion \(\texttt{g}+\texttt{g}\rightarrow\texttt{Q}\bar{\texttt{Q}}+\texttt{g}\). Before two gluons fuse into a stable quarkonium state, they acquire additional transverse momentum via the multi-scattering with their surrounding nucleons, such an extra transverse momentum would be inherited by the later formed quarkonium, thus leads to a broadening of transverse momentum distributions of quarkonium in proton+nucleus and nucleus+nucleus collisions with respect to what in proton+proton collisions. Such an effects are called the Cronin effect [37]. Another cold nuclear matter effect named "Nuclear" absorption [38, 39] may also play a role on the quarkonium suppression. It states that pre-quarkonia states or quarkonia states could dissociate through the interactions with their neighbor nuclei right after their formation. The probability \(\texttt{P}^{\texttt{s}}_{\texttt{abs}}\) for a quarkonia state to survive in a nucleus, for a p+A collision, can be expressed as \(\texttt{P}^{\texttt{s}}_{\texttt{abs}}=\texttt{exp}(-\texttt{\rho}_{\texttt{A }}\sigma_{\texttt{abs}}\texttt{L})\), where \(\texttt{\rho}_{\texttt{A}}\) is the nuclear density; \(\sigma_{\texttt{abs}}\) is the absorption cross section which stands for the probability of a quarkonia been broke up through the inelastic scattering with a nucleon; and L is the path length of a quarkonia inside the nuclear.

In order to explain the suppression of charmonium production and the enhancement of strangeness production in the nucleus+nucleus collisions at SPS, a theoretical approach named "Comover model" was developed in 1990s [40, 41, 42]. This model takes into account both mechanisms of dissociation and recombination for the quarkonium production, where the quarkoniumdissociation is assumed to be resulted from the interactions with the co-moving medium. The evolution of quarkonia can be expressed by a gain-and-loss differential equations:

\[\tau\frac{\text{dN}_{\text{QM}}}{\text{d}\tau}(\text{b},\text{s},\text{y})=- \sigma_{\text{co}}[\rho_{\text{med}}^{\text{co}}(\text{b},\text{s},\text{y}) \text{N}_{\text{QM}}(\text{b},\text{s},\text{y})-\text{N}_{\text{Q}}(\text{b}, \text{s},\text{y})\text{N}_{\text{\bar{Q}}}(\text{b},\text{s},\text{y})], \tag{1.5}\]

where, \(\tau\) is proper evolution time, b is the impact parameter, s is the squared center-of-mass energy, y is rapidity, \(\sigma_{\text{co}}\) is the cross section of quarkonium dissociation due to their interactions with the co-moving medium, \(\rho_{\text{med}}^{\text{co}}\) is the medium density. The first term of above equation corresponds to the quarkonium dissociation while the second term refers to the recombination of a Q\(\bar{\text{Q}}\) pair into a quarkonium state.

The survival probability of quarkonia can be obtained from the approximate solution of the above equation as:

\[\text{P}_{\text{co}}^{\text{S}}=\text{exp}[-\sigma_{\text{co}}[\rho_{\text{med }}^{\text{co}}(\text{b},\text{s},\text{y})-\frac{\text{N}_{\text{Q}}(\text{b}, \text{s},\text{y})\text{N}_{\text{\bar{Q}}}(\text{b},\text{s},\text{y})}{ \text{N}_{\text{QM}}}]\text{ln}[\frac{\rho_{\text{med}}^{\text{co}}(\text{b},\text{s},\text{y})}{\rho_{\text{pp}}(\text{y})}]\} \tag{1.6}\]

The interactions with medium will stop when the medium density is diluted to be same as the proton density. The suppression of quarkonium under the comover model frame thus depends on the medium density and the proper time of dilution. Without introducing any QGP effects, the comover model consistently described experimental measurements from RHIC to LHC energies. Although, today we learned that QGP played a dominant role on the quarkonium dissociation in heavy-ion collisions, the dissociation due to interactions with co-moving medium may still need to be taken into account.

