#### 3.1.3 Track Selection

As \(\Upsilon\) decays nearly at the collision point due to its very short lifetime, thus only primary tracks are used for the \(\Upsilon\) reconstruction. In order to select high quality primary tracks, the following basic cuts are applied:

* \(\mathrm{p_{T}}>0.2\) GeV/c, a cut on the transverse momentum of track, to ensure that the track can fly into the TPC volume under the STAR magnetic field.
* \(\mathrm{dc}a<1.5\) cm, a cut on the Distance of Closest Approach (Dca), to select those tracks close to primary tracks and reduce the secondary tracks.
* \(|\eta|<1\), a cut on the pseudorapidity, to ensure tracks stay within the TPC or EMC detector effective acceptance.
* nHitsFit \(\geq 25\), a cut on the number of hit points used to fit this track (the maximum given by the number of TPC readout pads, 45), to ensure the curve of a track is fitted by enough hit points so that a precise momentum information.
* \(\mathrm{R}\leq 0.52\), a cut on the ratio "\(\mathrm{R}\)" of hit points used for fitting over the maximum possible points along this track, to reduce the possibility of selecting duplicated short tracks.
* nHitsDedx \(\geq 15\), a cut on the number of points used for evaluating the energy loss \(<\mathrm{dE/dx}>\) of track, to ensure a precise \(\mathrm{dE/dx}\) information.

#### 3.1.4 Electron Track Selection

As \(\Upsilon\) are so massive (\(\mathrm{m}\Upsilon(1\mathrm{S})=9.46\) GeV/c\({}^{2}\)) that their decayed electrons are mainly high momentum electrons which can reach the BEMC detector (a threshold of \(\mathrm{E_{T}}\sim 1\) GeV).

Therefore, all electrons used for \(\Upsilon\) reconstruction in this analysis are required to be able to match to BEMC towers. The electron identification (EID) is done by making use of information from both TPC and BEMC detectors. The electron identification related cuts are as follows:

* \(\rm p_{T}>1.0\) GeV/c, a cut on the transverse momentum, to ensure the electron could reach BEMC detector.
* \(0.7<\rm E/p<2.0\), a cut on the ratio of energy deposited in BEMC tower over the momentum extracted based the curve of track and STAR magnetic field. As electrons deposit most of their energy in the BEMC towers, this ratio are expected to be close to 1, while on the other side hadrons deposit less energy in BEMC towers, thus this cut could help to reject hadrons.
* \(|z\rm Dist|<10\) cm, \(|\rm phiDist|<1\) cm, a cut on the difference between the BEMC recorded hit position and the extrapolated BEMC hit position based on TPC tracks. This is to ensure tracks are matched to BEMC towers.
* \(-1.5<\rm n\sigma_{e}<2.5\), \(\rm n\sigma_{e}=\frac{1}{\sigma(<dE/dx>)}\rm log\frac{<dE/dx>^{measured}}{<dE/dx>^{ expected}}\). \(<\rm dE/dx>^{measured}\) is the measured average energy loss of a track, \(<\rm dE/dx>^{expected}\) is the expected average energy loss while assuming this track is an electron track, \(\sigma(<\rm dE/dx>)\) is the energy loss resolution for electrons which depends on the path length, hit positions and momentum of a track. As different particle has different interaction property with the TPC gas, which results in different energy loss characteristic. Therefore the energy loss information measured by TPC could be used to perform the particle identification. However when momentum is high (\(\geq 3\) GeV/c), the bands of energy loss \(<\rm dE/dx>\) as a function of momentum of hadrons merge into what of electrons, making the discrimination of electrons and hadrons very difficult. Instead of using \(\mathrm{d}\mathrm{E}/\mathrm{d}\mathrm{x}\) directly, the new variable of \(\mathrm{n}\sigma_{\mathrm{c}}\), centering around zero with a width of unity for pure electrons, could provide a better power to separate electrons from hadrons, is thus more commonly used.
* \(\mathrm{D}\mathrm{s}\mathrm{m}\mathrm{A}\mathrm{d}\mathrm{c}>18\) & \(\mathrm{A}\mathrm{d}\mathrm{c}0>=300\) & \(\mathrm{p}_{\mathrm{T}}>3.5\) GeV/c, these cuts are only applied on the triggered electrons, where the trigger threshold values of \(\mathrm{D}\mathrm{s}\mathrm{m}\mathrm{A}\mathrm{d}\mathrm{c}\), \(\mathrm{A}\mathrm{d}\mathrm{c}\mathrm{0}\) and \(\mathrm{p}_{\mathrm{T}}\) corresponding to BHT2*BBCMB trigger is 18, 300 and \(\sim 3.5\) GeV/c. \(\mathrm{D}\mathrm{s}\mathrm{m}\mathrm{A}\mathrm{d}\mathrm{c}\) (or \(\mathrm{A}\mathrm{d}\mathrm{c}\mathrm{0}\)) is a variable converted from the deposited energy in the BEMC tower, which is used for the online trigger decision.

#### 3.1.5 Make Electron Pairs

After the selection of electron candidates, the \(\Upsilon\) candidates can be reconstructed based these electron candidates.The electrons from \(\Upsilon\) decays are always \(\mathrm{e}^{+}\mathrm{e}^{-}\) pairs, thus all the unlike-sign electron candidates within one event are made into pairs, called the unlike-sign pairs. However, these unlike-sign pairs not only includes the \(\Upsilon\) decayed electron pairs but also background contributions from semileptonic decays of correlated \(\mathrm{B}\)-\(\bar{\mathrm{B}}\) meson decays, \(\mathrm{D}\mathrm{r}\mathrm{e}\mathrm{l}-\mathrm{Y}\mathrm{a}\mathrm{n}\) process and random combinations.

The invariant mass of each pair can be calculated by:

\[\mathrm{m}_{\mathrm{e}\mathrm{e}}=\sqrt{(E_{1}+E_{2})^{2}-\|\overrightarrow{ \mathrm{p}_{1}}+\overrightarrow{\mathrm{p}_{2}}\|^{2}} \tag{3.1}\]where, for each electron candidate: the \(\overrightarrow{\rm p}\) is the momentum vector, which can be measured by TPC, \(\tt E=\sqrt{m_{e}^{2}+\overrightarrow{\rm p}^{\,2}}\) is the total kinematic energy; \(\tt m_{e}=0.511\) MeV/c\({}^{2}\) is the mass of electron.

* UnlikeSign Pairs = \(\Upsilon\)(1S) + \(\Upsilon\)(2S) + \(\Upsilon\)(3S) + B\(\bar{\rm B}\) + Drell\(-\)Yan + RandomCombination

The contribution from random combination can be estimated from the like-sign pairs (\(\rm e^{+}e^{+}\) or \(\rm e^{-}e^{-}\) ), and the physics background from semileptonic decays of correlated B-\(\bar{\rm B}\) meson decays and Drell-Yan process can be studied from the simulations. The yield of Upsilon signals can be obtained from the fitting of the subtraction of UnlikeSign and LikeSign electron pairs, and the details about the fitting method will be introduced in the later section.

Since \(\Upsilon\) has very large mass, their decayed electrons should be all high momentum electrons. As the mass of group \(\Upsilon\) state, \(\tt m_{\Upsilon(1S)}=9.46\)\(\rm GeV/c^{2}\), so the high momentum daughter electron should have a momentum larger than 4.5 \(\rm GeV/c\), and to further reduce the background level, a momentum cut on the lower momentum daughter electron is set to be 3.5 \(\rm GeV/c\). Since each event is triggered by the BHT2*BBCMB trigger, an additional requirement that there should be at least one triggered electron for each pair. The above two requirements are applied on both unlike-sign and like-sign pairs.

### 3.2 Efficiency Studies

#### 3.2.1 Single Electron Reconstruction Efficiency

The single electron reconstruction efficiency is studied mainly based on the STAR official embedding simulations [70]. For the STAR official embedding simulation, the Monte Carlo (MC) \(\Upsilon\) decayed electrons are feed into the GEANT3 [71] detector simulator with the full geometry of STAR detectors, where the interactions between particles and detector materials are simulated and generate the Monte Carlo response signals of detectors. These MC detector responses are embedded into the real BHT2*BBCMB triggered raw data which are randomly sampled over the entire available data samples. After the mixing of MC responses of detectors together with those from real data, the data reconstruction with exactly same algorithms as for the real data reconstruction are performed with these mixed responses or signals. The single electron reconstruction efficiency can be calculated by looking at the ratio of the number of successfully reconstructed electron tracks over the total number of input MC electron tracks.

The STAR official embedding simulations cannot well reproduce the \(\mathrm{n}\sigma_{\mathrm{e}}\) information of particles, thus the \(\mathrm{n}\sigma_{\mathrm{e}}\) cut efficiency of electrons is estimated from the real data. In real data, a pure electron sample is obtained from the photon conversion electrons (\(\mathrm{\gamma}\to\mathrm{e}^{+}\mathrm{e}^{-}\)), which are usually called photonic electrons. In this analysis, the photonic electrons are selected by the following selection criterion:

* the invariant mass of electron pairs: \(\mathrm{m}_{\mathrm{e}e}<0.24\)\(\mathrm{GeV/c}^{2}\)
* the Dca between two electron candidate tracks: \(\mathrm{Dca}_{\mathrm{e}e}<1.0\) cm
* tight cuts on one electron candidate: \(\mathrm{DsmAdc}>18\), \(\mathrm{Adc}0>300\), \(|\eta|<1\), \(\mathrm{p}_{\mathrm{T}}>0.2\), \(\mathrm{nHitsFit}\geq 20\), \(\mathrm{nHitsDedx}\geq 15\), \(\mathrm{R}>0.52\), \(-1<\mathrm{n}\sigma_{\mathrm{e}}<2.0\), \(\mathrm{Dca}<1.5\)cm, \(0.6<\mathrm{E/p}<3.0\), \(|\mathrm{zDist}|<3\), \(|\mathrm{phiDist}|<0.15\). This electron candidate is named as primary electron.
* cuts on the other electron candidate: \(|\eta|<1\), \(\mathrm{p}_{\mathrm{T}}>0.2\), \(\mathrm{nHitsFit}\geq 20\), \(\mathrm{nHitsDedx}\geq 15\), \(\mathrm{R}>0.52\). This electron candidate is named as partner electron.

With the above selection, a clean electron samples can be obtained. The distribution of \(\mathrm{n}\sigma_{\mathrm{e}}\) of electrons is studied by the partner electrons as there is no \(\mathrm{n}\sigma_{\mathrm{e}}\) cut on them. The left panel of Figure 38 shows an example of the \(\mathrm{n}\sigma_{\mathrm{e}}\) distribution of the partner electrons within a small \(\mathrm{p}_{\mathrm{T}}\) window of \(4.0<\mathrm{p}_{\mathrm{T}}<4.5\) GeV/c, the mean and width (sigma) of \(\mathrm{n}\sigma_{\mathrm{e}}\) can be extracted by fitting this \(\mathrm{n}\sigma_{\mathrm{e}}\) distribution with a Gaussian function. The mean and sigma as a function of \(\mathrm{p}_{\mathrm{T}}\) can be obtained as shown in the right panel of Figure 38, as they are nearly flat with \(\mathrm{p}_{\mathrm{T}}\), their values can be fitted by constant-value lines. The fitted constant mean and sigma of \(\mathrm{n}\sigma_{\mathrm{e}}\) can be set to a Gaussian function and then use this Gaussian function to perform the random sampling on the electron tracks for the estimation of the \(\mathrm{n}\sigma_{\mathrm{e}}\) cut efficiency on electrons.

Figure 38: The left panel shows an example of \(\mathrm{n}\sigma_{\mathrm{e}}\) distribution of photonic electrons within a transverse momentum window: \(4.0<\mathrm{p}_{\mathrm{T}}<4.5\) GeV/c. The black points corresponds to the unlike-sign pairs, the red points corresponds to the like-sign pairs, the purple points are obtained by subtracting the like-sign distribution from the unlike-sign distribution. The right panel shows the fitted mean and width (sigma) of \(\mathrm{n}\sigma_{\mathrm{e}}\) distributions as a function of \(\mathrm{p}_{\mathrm{T}}\).

The single electron efficiency in 2015 p+p and p+Au are calculated in different pseudorapidity bins, Figure 39 shows an example of single electron reconstruction efficiency as a function of \(\rm p_{T}\) within a pseudorapidity window of \(0.2<\eta<0.4\) in 2015 pp and pAu. Where, the efficiencies corresponding to TPC, EMC and the BHT2 trigger are defined as follows:

* "TPC" is defined by cuts of "\(\rm p_{T}>0.2\) GeV/c & nHitsFits \(\geq 25\) & nHitsDedx \(\geq 15\) & \(\rm R\geq 0.52\) & \(0<\rm Dca<1.5\) cm & \(-1.5<n\sigma_{e}<2.0\)" The efficiency of TPC: \(\rm\epsilon_{TPC}=\frac{TPC}{MC}\)
