### 3.3 Upsilon Yields Extraction

The extraction of \(\Upsilon\) signal yields is performed based a powerful package: RooFit, which provides a tookit for modeling the expected distribution of events in a physics analysis.

The subtraction of unlike-sign and like-sign electron pairs are fitted by a function composed of \(\Upsilon(1S)\), \(\Upsilon(2S)\), \(\Upsilon(3S)\) shapes, BB shape and DY shape:

\[\begin{split}\text{f}_{\text{total}}=&\text{Yield}(1S)* \epsilon(1S)*\pdf(1S)+\text{Yield}(2S)*\epsilon(2S)*\pdf(2S)+\text{Yield}(3S)* \epsilon(3S)*\pdf(3S)\\ &+\text{Yield}(BB)*\epsilon(BB)*\pdf(BB)+\text{Yield}(DY)*\epsilon( DY)*\pdf(DY)\end{split} \tag{3.4}\]

Where:

* "Yield" is the yield corrected by detection efficiency
* "\(\epsilon\)" is the detection efficiency
* "\(\pdf\)" is the particle probability distribution function

Because of the resolution in our data does not allow a clear separation of individual \(\Upsilon\) state from each other, the three \(\Upsilon\) pdfs are combined into one single pdf in our fitting. The combined pdf for all \(\Upsilon\) states \(\Upsilon(1S2S3S)\) can be constructed from each \(\Upsilon\) pdf while weighted by their detection efficiency and the world wide experimental average ratios of \(\Upsilon(1S):\Upsilon(2S):\Upsilon(3S)=1:0.275:0.128\) from PDG.

The combined Upsilon pdf can be expressed as:

\[\begin{split}\text{TT}&=\text{Yield}(\text{1S})*\epsilon( \text{1S})*\text{pdf}(\text{1S})+\text{Yield}(\text{2S})*\epsilon(\text{2S})* \text{pdf}(\text{2S})+\text{Yield}(\text{3S})*\epsilon(\text{3S})*\text{pdf}( \text{3S})\\ &=\frac{\text{Yield}(\text{1S2S3S})*\epsilon(\text{1S2S3S})}{\text{ Yield}(\text{1S2S3S})*\epsilon(\text{1S2S3S})}*\text{TT}\\ &=\frac{\text{Yield}(\text{1S2S3S})*\epsilon(\text{1S2S3S})}{\text{ Yield}(\text{1S2S3S})*\epsilon(\text{1S2S3S})}*\{\text{Yield}(\text{Y}( \text{1S}))*\epsilon(\text{Y}(\text{1S}))*\text{pdf}(\text{Y}(\text{1S}))\\ &+\text{Yield}(\text{Y}(\text{2S}))*\epsilon(\text{Y}(\text{2S}))* \text{pdf}(\text{Y}(\text{2S}))+\text{Yield}(\text{Y}(\text{3S}))*\epsilon( \text{Y}(\text{3S}))*\text{pdf}(\text{Y}(\text{3S}))\}\\ &=\text{Yield}(\text{1S2S3S})*\epsilon(\text{1S2S3S})*\{\frac{ \text{Yield}(\text{1S})}{\text{Yield}(\text{1S2S3S})}*\frac{\epsilon(\text{1S} )}{\epsilon(\text{1S2S3S})}*\text{pdf}(\text{1S})\\ &+\frac{\text{Yield}(\text{2S})}{\text{Yield}(\text{1S2S3S})}* \frac{\epsilon(\text{2S})}{\epsilon(\text{1S2S3S})}*\text{pdf}(\text{2S})+ \frac{\text{Yield}(\text{3S})}{\text{Yield}(\text{1S2S3S})}*\frac{\epsilon( \text{3S})}{\epsilon(\text{1S2S3S})}*\text{pdf}(\text{3S})\}\end{split} \tag{3.5}\]

Where:

* \(\text{Yield}(\text{1S2S3S})=\text{Yield}(\text{1S})+\text{Yield}(\text{2S})+ \text{Yield}(\text{3S})\)
* \(\epsilon(\text{1S2S3S})=\frac{\text{Yield}(\text{1S})}{\text{Yield}(\text{1S2 S3S})}*\epsilon(\text{1S})+\frac{\text{Yield}(\text{2S})}{\text{Yield}(\text{1S2S3S})}* \epsilon(\text{2S})+\frac{\text{Yield}(\text{3S})}{\text{Yield}(\text{1S2S3S}) }*\epsilon(\text{3S})\)
* \(\frac{\text{Yield}(\text{1S})}{\text{Yield}(\text{1S2S3S})}=0.7042,\ \frac{\text{Yield}(\text{2S})}{\text{Yield}(\text{1S2S3S})}=0.2042,\ \frac{\text{Yield}(\text{3S})}{\text{Yield}(\text{1S2S3S})}=0.0915\)

The fitting function can be transformed to be as follows:

\[\begin{split}\text{f}_{\text{total}}&=\text{Yield} (\text{1S2S3S})*\epsilon(\text{1S2S3S})*\{\text{c}_{1}*\text{pdf}(\text{1S})+ \text{c}_{2}*\text{pdf}(\text{2S})+\text{c}_{3}*\text{pdf}(\text{3S})\}\\ &+\text{Yield}(\text{BB})*\epsilon(\text{BB})*\text{pdf}(\text{ BB})+\text{Yield}(\text{DY})*\epsilon(\text{DY})*\text{pdf}(\text{DY})\end{split} \tag{3.6}\]

Where:

* \(\text{Yield}(\text{1S2S3S})\) is the efficiency corrected yield of \(\text{Y}(\text{1S2S3S})\)* \(\epsilon(\text{1S2S3S})\) is the efficiency of \(\Upsilon(\text{1S2S3S})\)
* \(\text{c}_{i}\) is the constant of \(\frac{\text{Yield(iS)}}{\text{Yield(1S2S3S)}}*\frac{\epsilon(\text{iS})}{\epsilon( \text{1S2S3S})}\), \(\text{i}=\text{1,2,3}\)

In the fitting, \(\text{pdf(1S)}\), \(\text{pdf(2S)}\), \(\text{pdf(3S)}\), BB, DY shapes are obtained from the simulation, \(\text{Yield(1S2S3S)}*\epsilon(\text{1S2S3S})\), \(\text{Yield(BB)}*\epsilon(\text{BB})\) and \(\text{Yield(DY)}*\epsilon(\text{DY})\) are free parameters (as raw yields) which will be obtained by fitting to the invariant mass spectrum (UnlikeSign - LikeSign) in real data.

