#### 3.4.2 In Bunch Pileup Effects Study

Due to the very high luminosity with 2015 p+p and p+Au collisions, the effects of more than one collisions (in-bunch pile up effects) happened during a bunch crossing may not be ignored. To quantify this effects, we may start from assuming that \(\lambda\) is the probability that one collision happens during a given bunch-crossing. Then the probability of that there are k collisions happen in a bunch-crossing would be:

\[P_{k}[k\text{ collisions in a bunch-crossing}]=\frac{\lambda^{k}e^{- \lambda}}{k!}\]

and we would have: \(P_{0}=e^{-\lambda}\), \(P_{1}=\lambda e^{-\lambda}\), \(P_{2}=\frac{\lambda^{2}}{2}e^{-\lambda}\) and \(P_{3}=\frac{\lambda^{3}}{6}e^{-\lambda}\). The contribution of \(k\geq 3\) is much smaller compared to k=1 and k=2, thus ignored in our study.

Let's start from the in-bunch pile up study for p+p collisions, if let \(\epsilon\) be the BBC single side fired efficiency(\(\epsilon\sim 0.9\)), we could classify the BBC triggered events as the following cases:

* For k=1, the probability of that two sides of BBC are fired: \[P_{\text{BBC}}[k=1]=P_{1}*\epsilon^{2}\] (3.10)
* For k=2, two sides of BBC are fired can be due to following 4 cases: 1. two collisions both fire two sides of BBC 2. one collision fire two sides of BBC and another one fire only one side of BBC 3. one collision fire two sides of BBC and another one fired nothing 4. one collision fire one side of BBC and the other collision fire the other side of BBCThe probability of that two sides of BBC fired:

\[\text{P}_{\text{BBC}}[\text{k}=2]=\text{P}_{2}*[\epsilon^{4}+4*\epsilon^{3}(1- \epsilon)+2*\epsilon^{2}(1-\epsilon)^{2}+2*\epsilon^{2}(1-\epsilon)^{2}]=\text{P} _{2}*[\epsilon^{2}(2-\epsilon)^{2}] \tag{3.11}\]

The fraction of k=2 events among all BBC triggered events can be obtained by

\[\text{Fraction}(\text{k}=2)=\frac{\text{P}_{\text{BBC}}[\text{k}=2]}{\text{P} _{\text{BBC}}[\text{k}=1]+\text{P}_{\text{BBC}}[\text{k}=2]}=\frac{\text{P}_{2 }*[\epsilon^{2}(2-\epsilon)^{2}]}{\text{P}_{2}*[\epsilon^{2}(2-\epsilon)^{2}]+ \text{P}_{1}*\epsilon^{2}}=\frac{\lambda(2-\epsilon)^{2}}{2+\lambda(2- \epsilon)^{2}} \tag{3.12}\]

The BBC rate in per bunch-crossing due to the real collisions should be the expectation of k=1 and k=2 collisions, then we have the following relation:

\[\frac{\text{BBCRate}}{9.838\text{MHz}*\frac{102}{120}}=1*\text{P}_{1}*\epsilon ^{2}+2*\text{P}_{2}*\epsilon^{2}(2-\epsilon)^{2}+...\cong\lambda\text{e}^{- \lambda}\epsilon^{2}+\lambda^{2}\text{e}^{-\lambda}\epsilon^{2}(2-\epsilon)^{2} \tag{3.13}\]

\[\frac{\text{BBCRate}/\epsilon^{2}}{9.838\text{MHz}*\frac{102}{120}}=\lambda \text{e}^{-\lambda}+\lambda^{2}\text{e}^{-\lambda}(2-\epsilon)^{2}\cong\lambda\text { (while $\lambda$ is small and $\epsilon$ close to 1)} \tag{3.14}\]For the in-bunch pile up in p+Au collisions, the similar derivation procedures as in p+p can be performed. Let \(\epsilon_{\text{E}}\) and \(\epsilon_{\text{W}}\) be the efficiency of BBC east and BBC west, we could have:

\[\text{Fraction}(\text{k}=2) =\frac{\text{P}_{\text{BBC}}[\text{k}=2]}{\text{P}_{\text{BBC}}[ \text{k}=1]+\text{P}_{\text{BBC}}[\text{k}=2]}=\frac{\text{P}_{2}*[\epsilon_{ \text{E}}*\epsilon_{\text{W}}*(\epsilon_{\text{W}}-2)(\epsilon_{\text{E}}-2)]}{ \text{P}_{2}*[\epsilon_{\text{E}}*\epsilon_{\text{W}}*(\epsilon_{\text{W}}-2)( \epsilon_{\text{E}}-2)]+\text{P}_{1}*\epsilon_{\text{E}}*\epsilon_{\text{W}}}\] \[=\frac{\lambda(2-\epsilon_{\text{E}})*(2-\epsilon_{\text{W}})}{2 +\lambda(2-\epsilon_{\text{E}})*(2-\epsilon_{\text{W}})} \tag{3.15}\]

\[\frac{\text{BBCRate}/(\epsilon_{\text{E}}*\epsilon_{\text{W}})}{9.838\text{ MHz}*\frac{102}{120}}=\lambda*\text{e}^{-\lambda}+\lambda^{2}*\text{e}^{-\lambda}*(2- \epsilon_{\text{E}})*(2-\epsilon_{W})\cong\lambda \tag{3.16}\]

The \(\lambda\) and \(\text{Fraction}[\text{k}=2]\) can be obtained in the real data. The \(\lambda\) could be obtained based on the BBC rates in each run. The \(\lambda\) and \(\text{frac}[\text{k}=2]\) distributions in Run15pp and Run15pAu are shown in Figure 51. The average of \(\text{Fraction}[\text{k}=2]\) for all runs can be calculated:

\[<\text{Fraction}[\text{k}=2]>=\frac{\sum_{\text{firstRun}}^{\text{lastRun}} \text{eqv}.\text{MB}[\text{iRun}]*\text{Fraction}(\text{iRun})}{\sum_{\text{ firstRun}}^{\text{lastRun}}\text{eqv}.\text{MB}[\text{iRun}]} \tag{3.17}\]

The final equivalent number of MB events are calculated by:

\[\text{N}_{\text{MB}}^{\text{eqv}.}(\text{final})=\text{N}_{\text{MB}}^{\text {eqv}.}*(1+<\text{frac}[\text{k}=2]>) \tag{3.18}\]

The in-bunch pile up effects does not only contribute to the number of equivalent MB events, but also contribute to the corresponding BBC efficiency and vertex reconstruction efficiency. We will discuss how to take it into account on the trigger bias factors in the following sections.

The final number of equivalent MB events for Run15 pp and Run15 pAu BHT2*BBCMB events are summarized and listed in Table 7.

