### **Physics Results**

The invariant yield of Upsilon can be obtained by the following formula:

\[\text{Inv.Yield}(\Upsilon)=\frac{1}{\text{N}_{\text{MB}}^{\text{eqv.}}\epsilon_{ \text{MB}}^{\text{goodvtx}}}*\frac{\text{N}_{\Upsilon}^{\text{raw}}}{\text{ Acc.}\times\epsilon^{\text{Trig}}\epsilon^{\text{trk}}\epsilon^{\text{el \text{ID}}}}*\frac{\epsilon_{\text{MB}}^{\text{ZDC}}\epsilon_{\text{MB}}^{ \text{goodvtx}}}{\epsilon_{\Upsilon}^{\text{ZDC}}\epsilon_{\Upsilon}^{\text{ goodvtx}}} \tag{4.3}\]

Figure 72: Systematic uncertainties from different sources for \(\text{R}_{\text{AA}}\) of \(\Upsilon\)(1S+2S+3S), \(\Upsilon\)(1S), \(\Upsilon\)(2S+3S) as a function of \(\text{p}_{\text{T}}\) in 0-60% centrality within rapidity ranges: \(|y|<1\) from 2011 Au+Au collisions and 2015 p+p collisions

Here, the above equation is basically same as what we used for the invariant yield calculation Upsilon in p+Au calculation or identical to the cross section calculation in p+p collisions. However, a much larger multiplicity in the Au+Au collisions makes differences of ZDC coincidence and the good vertex efficiency between Upsilon events and normal MB events can be negligible, thus \(\epsilon_{\gamma}^{\text{ZDC}}=\epsilon_{\text{MB}}^{\text{ZDC}}\), and with the same reason, the good vertex efficiency in Au+Au is 1.

\[\text{Inv.Yield}(\gamma)=\frac{1}{N_{\text{MB}}^{\text{eqv.}}\epsilon_{\text{ MB}}^{\text{goodvtx}}}*\frac{N_{\gamma}^{\text{raw}}}{Acc.\times e^{\text{ Trig}}\epsilon^{\text{trk}}\epsilon^{\text{e}\text{i}\text{D}}} \tag{4.4}\]

The nuclear modification factor \(R_{\text{AA}}\) is defined as follows:

\[R_{\text{AA}}=\frac{\text{Inv.Yield}_{\text{AA}}^{(\gamma)}}{\sigma_{\text{pp} }^{(\gamma)}}*\frac{\sigma_{\text{pp}}^{\text{inel.}}}{<N_{\text{coll}}^{ \text{AA}}>} \tag{4.5}\]

Note that:

* \(N_{\text{MB}}^{\text{eqv.}}\) is the number of equivalent ZDC MB events within \(|V_{z}|<30\) cm.
* \(\epsilon_{\text{MB}}^{\text{goodvtx}}\) is the good vertex efficiency defined by the fraction of events within \(V_{z}\) satisfying the condition of "\(|V_{z}^{\text{TPC}}-V_{z}^{\text{VPD}}|<3\) cm and \(\sqrt{V_{x}^{2}+V_{y}^{2}}<2\) cm".
* \(\sigma_{\text{pp}}^{(\gamma)}\) is the Upsilon cross section measured in 2015 p+p collisions.
* \(\sigma_{\text{pp}}^{\text{inel.}}\) is the inelastic cross section of p+p collisions.
* \(<N_{\text{coll}}^{\text{AA}}>\) is the average number of binary collisions in Au+Au collisions, as listed in Table 11.

In this analysis, Upsilon is measured via \(\Upsilon\to\mathrm{e}^{+}\mathrm{e}^{-}\) decay channel, the corresponding physics results are shown in section 4.7.1. Since the full installation of MTD at STAR in 2014, the di-muon trigger has collected large data sets in 2014 and 2016 Au+Au collisions at \(\sqrt{\mathrm{s}_{\mathrm{NN}}}=200\) GeV, with integrated luminosity of \(\mathcal{L}_{2014}=14.2\) nb\({}^{-1}\) and \(\mathcal{L}_{2016}=12.8\) nb\({}^{-1}\), respectively. The results of \(\mathrm{R}_{\mathrm{AA}}^{\Upsilon(1S)}\) and the ratio \(\frac{\Upsilon(2S+3S)}{\Upsilon(1S)}\) from this analysis (measured in \(\Upsilon\to\mathrm{e}^{+}\mathrm{e}^{-}\) decay channel) and di-muon analysis (measured in \(\Upsilon\to\mathrm{\mu}^{+}\mathrm{\mu}^{-}\) decay channel) are consistent with each other as shown in section 4.7.2, thus the results from these two analysis are combined together and the physics conclusions will be draw based on the combined results.

